  <div id="sutta">
  
              <h2 id="SN1.9" class="h1">Fond of Conceit<br/>
                Manakāma Sutta  (SN 1:9)</h2>
              <p>Standing to one side, a devatā addressed the Blessed One with a verse:</p>
              <div class="verse">
                <p>“Here there’s no taming</p>
                <p>for one fond of conceit,</p>
                <p>no sagacity</p>
                <p>for one unconcentrated.</p>
                <p>One dwelling alone in the wilderness</p>
                <p class="v1">heedlessly</p>
                <p>won’t cross over beyond</p>
                <p>Deaths’ realm.</p>
                <div class="verse-add">
                  <p class="vspk">The Buddha:</p>
                  <p>“Abandoning conceit,</p>
                  <p>his mind well-concentrated,</p>
                  <p>well-aware, everywhere</p>
                  <p class="v1">released,<span class="fn"><a href="#sn1.9note01">1</a></span><!--end fn cite--></p>
                  <p>one dwelling alone in the wilderness</p>
                  <p class="v1">heedfully:</p>
                  <p><em>He</em> will cross over beyond</p>
                  <p>Death’s realm.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="note">
                <p class="notetitle">Note</p><!--end notetitle-->
                <p id="sn1.9note01">1. For a discussion of the implications of this phrase, see <em>The Paradox of Becoming,</em> chapter 7.</p>
              </div><!--end notes-->
      </div><!--end:sutta-->
    </div><!--end:content-->
    
